":["Single-player","Steam Achievements","Family Sharing"],"platforms":{"windows":true,"mac":false,"linux":false},"controller":"full","dev":["Pendulo Studios"],"pub":["Microids"],"released":"Dec 16, 2021","meta":null,"langs":["English","French","Italian","German","Spanish - Spain","Russian"],"min":[["OS","Windows 7 or later, 64-bit"],["Processor","Intel Core i5 (3GHz) 4\u00ba generation or equivalent"],["Memory","8 GB RAM"],["Graphics","Nvidia GTX 960 or AMD R9 285 2GB"],["DirectX","Version 11"],["Storage","22 GB available space"]],"rec":[["OS","Window 10 (64-bit OS required)"],["Processor","Intel i7 (9\u00ba gen) (>3GHz)"],["Memory","16 GB RAM"],["Graphics","Nvidia GTX 1060 6GB or AMD Radeon RX 580"],["DirectX","Version 12"],["Storage","22 GB available space"],["Additional Notes","SSD recommended"]],"deck":2,"blurb":"Writer Ed Miller survives a car crash in a California canyon and insists his family was with him in the vehicle, though the wreck holds no sign of them. He develops severe vertigo, and sessions with a therapist become his route toward what really happened. You play through the investigation from the viewpoints of three different characters, each with a story of their own.\n\nThe tone is unsettling and psychological, with themes of obsession, control and doubt about what is real. It echoes the look and narrative devices of classic suspense films and takes loose inspiration from Hitchcock's movie rather than retelling it. It suits single-player adventure fans who like slow-building dread."}}